  "version": 1,
  "_comment": "Wallet-derived blinding for anonymous-token batches (warren-core doc 103 section 11, PROD-READINESS section 7). The issuer serves the SAME batch again to any client of the account that sends it bit for bit, so every client of a wallet must derive the identical batch: blinding_key = HKDF-SHA256(ikm = seed32, salt = \"warren/token-blinding/v1\", info = purpose, 32 bytes), where seed32 is the first 32 bytes of the BIP39 seed (empty passphrase), the same seed the identity derives from. Slot `index` of `epoch` draws its bytes from a stream: draw k of length L is the concatenation of HKDF-SHA256(ikm = blinding_key, salt = \"warren/token-blinding/v1\", info = epoch u64 BE || index u32 BE || block u32 BE, 32 bytes) for consecutive blocks, truncated to L; block starts at 0 for each slot and keeps counting across draws, and every draw starts on a fresh block. A slot draws, in this order: the 32-byte token nonce, the 48-byte PSS salt, then 256 bytes per attempt at the blinding factor r = OS2IP(draw) mod n, retried while r <= 1 or r is not invertible mod n. The number and order of draws are part of the contract. blinded = EMSA-PSS-ENCODE(token_input, salt) * r^e mod n (RFC 9474 RSABSSA-SHA384-PSS-Deterministic), token_input = 0x0002 || nonce || challenge_digest || token_key_id. blind_signature is what the issuer answers (blinded^d mod n, deterministic); token = token_input || blind_signature * r^-1 mod n. The issuer key is the synthetic warrenguard-token test key; the mnemonic is the well-known all-abandon BIP39 test phrase. Generated by running the warren-sdk-ts reference (blindingKeyFromSeed, deterministicTokenRandom, mintEpoch), independently of the Rust port that replays it.",
